{
  "abstract": "We prove large-value bounds for distinct cubic conductor-pair characters and derive moment, zero-density, and selected-correlation estimates. For $\\alpha_{x,y}=\\rho_x\\overline{\\rho_y}$ with coprime squarefree endpoints of norm lengths $X,Y$, put $C=\\sqrt{XY}$. The squared off-diagonal Gram row sum at ordinary length $Z$ is at most $(CZ)^\\epsilon(C^2Z+C^5)$. The proof retains the conductor pair through primitive Poisson, using de Faveri's conductor inequality with common dual coefficients and all overlap masks.\n\nA finite transfer sends a large-value monomial $C^aT^bV^{-q}$ to $C^{a+4b}T^{j(q-b)}V^{-2jq}$, together with $T^j/V^j$, for fixed integers $j\\ge2$ under stated exponent conditions. A third-power estimate gives the fourth moment $Q^{13/10+\\epsilon}$ for the specified primitive cubic Hecke family of conductor at most $Q$, uniformly at bounded height. Applying the transfers to an actual saturated zero-detector factor gives an explicit density exponent throughout $3/4<\\sigma<1$, at a sufficiently small positive height power. At $\\sigma=5/6$ the exponent is $3/5$, compared with $3/4$ from the displayed conductor second-moment detector.\n\nTwo fifth-power transfers and existing same-block comparisons give a complete nine-term bound for a selected two-mark Eisenstein correlation. At inverse length $D$ and mark length $D^{15/16}$, the quadratic-core cutoff increases from $D^{23/48}$ to $D^{634/1263}$, with a fixed saving below the endpoint. CPU controls check finite identities and exact exponent algebra. Complementary cores and signed different-shift covariance remain unresolved; no individual zero-free region or RH resolution is claimed.",
